<p>Bayan is a value-oriented language that compiles to native code, with no garbage collector, no runtime and no exceptions. This page gives the decisions behind that, the reason for each and its cost; the specification has a rationale note for every decision, and marks a feature the compiler lacks as "planned", with a fragment, not a program.</p>

<h2 id="principles">The principles</h2>
<p>The specification opens with this list:</p>
<ul>
<li>Performance is a feature: allocation, suspension and failure are always visible in the source or tooling.</li>
<li>Every keystroke carries meaning: type what carries meaning; the compiler infers the rest reliably.</li>
<li>One spelling per construct.</li>
<li>Memory safety is never a build-mode option.</li>
<li>Promise as little as possible: anything specified exactly is permanent, so leave room where it costs nothing.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="one-form">One form, one meaning</h2>
<p><strong>What it is.</strong> Two spellings of one construct are not allowed: one is canonical, the other an "input form" the compiler recognises, reports and offers a fix for. There is no <code>;</code>, <code>/* */</code> comment or <code>foreach</code>; each gives an error saying what to write instead.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Same thing, same way: easier to read and search, and a formatter has nothing to argue about.</p>
<p>Different compositions that mean the same thing are allowed (<code>users except null</code>, <code>users.where(it is not null)</code>), as is a recoverable part left out: <code>it</code>, an inferred type, a target-typed <code>.member</code>.</p>
<p><strong>What it costs.</strong> Some C-family habits stop working. Two documented exceptions, picked once per project in <code>package.byn</code>: boolean operators (<code>&amp;&amp;</code> or <code>and</code>) and primitive type names (<code>i32</code> or <code>int</code>).</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let line = a + b                 // BYN2209: strings cannot be joined with `+`
let line = $"{a}{b}"             // the one way to join text</code></pre>

<h2 id="keystrokes">Every keystroke carries meaning</h2>
<p><strong>What it is.</strong> Newlines end statements, conditions have no parentheses, and a type is written only where it adds information. Recoverable parts are left out: a target-typed <code>.red</code> for <code>Color.red</code>, <code>it</code> in a one-argument lambda. Parts that matter are written: mutation is <code>ref this</code>, ownership transfer is <code>move</code>, an argument's role a particle such as <code>from:</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A call site should say what it does: <code>transfer(200, from: aisha, to: maryam)</code> cannot swap its two accounts without the labels changing.</p>
<p><strong>What it costs.</strong> More to learn up front: a function with several required parameters needs a particle on all but one, repeated at every call. The <a href="reading.html">reading guide</a> goes through the unfamiliar forms.</p>
<example name="features-particles"/>

<h2 id="errors-values">Errors are values, not exceptions</h2>
<p><strong>What it is.</strong> A failing function says so in its signature: <code>i32 reserve(...) fails StockError</code>. Inside it, <code>fail .soldOut</code> leaves with an error and <code>return</code> always returns a value. A caller propagates with <code>try</code> or handles it with <code>catch</code>; nothing can be caught the signature did not declare. A panic is different: it means a bug and aborts the program.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> An exception can come from any call without warning; a <code>fails</code> type keeps failure visible in the source and costs the same as a return value.</p>
<p><strong>What it costs.</strong> A public function must list its errors, and every failing call needs <code>try</code>. <a href="errors.html">Errors</a> covers the full set of forms.</p>
<example name="features-errors"/>

<h2 id="no-gc">No garbage collector and no runtime</h2>
<p><strong>What it is.</strong> Data is made of structs, values living on the stack, in an arena, on the heap through an allocator, or in static data. Collections and other resources are <code>unique</code>: assigning one moves it, and a copy is asked for. Borrows are checked against their region, and lifetime annotations are rare.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Memory costs are in the source, and a program starts with nothing running beside it.</p>
<p><strong>What it costs.</strong> You decide where memory comes from and who owns it. A borrow cannot outlive its source, and a value cannot be changed structurally while a borrow into it is alive; the fix is usually <code>.copy()</code>. A garbage collector is a possible future allocator, never a language mode. <a href="memory.html">Memory and ownership</a> has the details.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">var a = List&lt;i32&gt;()
a.append(1)
let b = move a        // `a` can no longer be used
var c = b.copy()      // an explicit copy</code></pre>

<h2 id="effects">Effects in signatures</h2>
<p><strong>What it is.</strong> A public function lists what it does besides computing: <code>uses alloc</code>, <code>uses net</code>, <code>uses fs</code>. The list is closed: <code>alloc suspend net fs clock random env process console</code>. No <code>uses</code> clause means pure; private functions infer their effects. An undeclared effect on a public function is an error (BYN6002).</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A signature is a contract: it tells you whether a call allocates, touches the network or waits on I/O. A package can declare which capabilities it may use; without <code>.net</code> it cannot contain a function that uses it.</p>
<p><strong>What it costs.</strong> Public signatures get longer, and a new effect reaches the public callers. A function-typed parameter takes the effects of whatever you pass, so <code>map</code> needs no separate variants.</p>
<example name="features-effects"/>
<p>Removing <code>uses alloc</code> from <code>squares</code> gives this error from the compiler:</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Compiler output">error BYN6002: `squares` uses `alloc` but does not declare it</code></pre>

<h2 id="safety">Safety modes</h2>
<p><strong>What it is.</strong> Build modes: <code>debug</code>, <code>safe</code>, <code>fast</code> and <code>small</code>; the command line accepts <code>--mode debug|safe|fast</code> today. Modes change how much checking and diagnosis you get, never whether the program is memory safe: bounds checks and integer-overflow traps apply in every mode, as do <code>requires</code> checks and <code>expects</code> contracts, and the wrapping and saturating operators (<code>+%</code>, <code>+|</code>) are separate spellings.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A program that behaves differently in <code>fast</code> makes the fast build a different program; here only the extra diagnosis changes. Debug builds check ownership at run time and report a second owner or a use after a move, with both source locations.</p>
<p><strong>What it costs.</strong> Some checks cost time, and the compiler removes the provably redundant ones. Outside <code>unsafe</code> there is no undefined behaviour. Raw pointers, pointer arithmetic and <code>extern</code> calls need an <code>unsafe because "…"</code> block, and <code>bayan audit</code> is specified to list each bypass with its reason. Today <code>bayan audit &lt;path&gt;</code> prints each <code>trustUnchecked() because</code> bypass with its file, position, reason and count. The <code>unsafe</code> block, the other <code>because</code> forms and the <code>extern</code> listing are planned.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let ok: string = typed.trustUnchecked() because "demo input"</code></pre>

<h2 id="c-backend">Compiling to C through zig cc</h2>
<p><strong>What it is.</strong> The compiler checks your program, turns it into C, and runs <code>zig cc</code> on the result: an ordinary native executable. The emitted C defines every behaviour C leaves undefined, with explicit checks and the flags <code>-fwrapv</code> and <code>-ffp-contract=off</code>. <code>bayan build --emit-c</code> shows the C.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> One mature C compiler gives many targets and good code, with no back end per target, and <code>zig cc</code> cross-compiles from one machine. Targets are <code>&lt;os&gt;-&lt;arch&gt;</code>, os <code>linux</code>/<code>macos</code>/<code>windows</code>, arch <code>x64</code>/<code>arm64</code>, plus <code>wasm32</code>.</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Shell">bayan build main.byn --emit-c        # show the C
bayan build main.byn --target linux-x64</code></pre>
<p><strong>What it costs.</strong> You need <code>zig</code> on <code>PATH</code> or a C compiler in <code>BAYAN_CC</code>, and compile time includes a C compiler run. The specification plans a faster in-process path for debug builds and scripts, not yet done.</p>

<h2 id="self-hosting">Self-hosting</h2>
<p><strong>What it is.</strong> The compiler is written in Bayan and compiles itself. Its source is in <code>src/</code>, and <code>bootstrap/bayan.c</code> holds the same compiler as one C file, enough to build from scratch with a C compiler.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A language good for compilers should write one, and doing so finds gaps while they are cheap to fix. The site you are reading is generated by a Bayan program.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">// part of the lexer, written in Bayan
bool isDigit(u8 c) =&gt; c &gt;= '0' &amp;&amp; c &lt;= '9'</code></pre>
<p><strong>What it costs.</strong> The compiler implements a subset of the specification; features it has not needed yet, such as units and some collection methods, come later.</p>
